    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. That heat travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge and escapes into your kitchen. Next, the refrigerant cools down, turns back into a liquid, and cycles through again. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold. The fridge doesn’t “create” cold—it just moves heat out, and insulation helps keep it out."
